.TH SITU-EDIT 1 "" "situ" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
situ-edit \- read and edit a message against a situ schema
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B situ-edit
.RI [ options ]
.I schema
.I message
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B situ-edit
reads
.I message
as the schema describes it and prints each field, its offset, its value
and what the schema says about it. Given edits, it applies them where the
schema permits one and shows the result; nothing is written unless
.B \-\-out
names where.
.PP
.I schema
is a
.I .situ
schema, a packed image, or a directory of schemas, in which case the file
is recognised by their
.B identify
declarations.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-struct " STRUCT"
Which layout to read the message as; the first by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-offset " N"
Where the struct starts in the file.
.TP
.BI \-\-length " N"
How long its frame is; the rest of the file by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-within " STRUCT:OFFSET:LENGTH"
A window this one was opened inside, outermost first, so a byte run may
change length inside a nested struct.
.TP
.BI \-\-relate " NAME"
Check a cross-message relation by name, with
.BI \-\-against " FILE"
as the later message.
.TP
.BI \-\-arg " N"
A value for one of the struct's
.B parameter
members, in declaration order; repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-hex
The message file holds hex rather than bytes.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " " text | json
The output's form; json is for another frontend.
.TP
.BI \-\-set " NAME=VALUE"
Store a value, decimal or 0x hex; repeatable, applied in order.
.TP
.BI \-\-set\-bytes " NAME=HEX"
Store a byte run; a run written shorter or longer moves what follows.
.TP
.BI \-\-put " NAME=TEXT"
Store text read the way the field shows it.
.TP
.BI \-\-insert " NAME:N=HEX"
Put an element into a run of structs, moving the field that sizes it.
.TP
.BI \-\-remove " NAME:N"
Take an element out of a run of structs.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " FILE"
Write the edited message here.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR situc (1),
.BR situ\-edit\-tui (1),
.BR situ\-edit\-gui (1),
.BR situ\-walk (1)
